    description: "FOUND 2026-09-04 by the docstring-staleness audit. The text was fixed then (PR #776) and hand-extended since; the DIVERGENCE that produced it is not fixed, and it has recurred.\n\nTHE SHAPE. `hypergumbo verify-claims --help` renders `verify_claims_epilog`, a hand-written string in cli.py (~L11163; enumeration ~L11170-11177) that enumerates the legal `boundary:` values and then asserts:\n\n    \"a boundary value outside the vocabulary above produces a clear error and exit code 2\"\n\nThe validator does not read that list. It derives its own answer from `IO_BOUNDARY_TYPES` (io_boundary_types.py ~L156) and prints a second, independently-generated enumeration in its error message. Nothing in cli.py references IO_BOUNDARY_TYPES. So one subcommand ships two vocabularies for one axis, and only one of them is attached to the registry that changes.\n\nMEASURED at dev 134a06d505 (2026-09-30): the registry names 21 boundaries; the help lists 20. Missing from the help: `navigation_read`, added to the registry 2026-09-29 in 8f2178a437 (INV-dadu). Nothing in the help is absent from the registry.\n\n    boundary: navigation_read      -> ACCEPTED by the validator (absent from the help list)\n    boundary: not_a_real_boundary  -> \"Error: unknown boundary ...; valid boundaries:\n                                      <all 21 enumerated>\", rc 2\n\nAt filing the same drift was two values (command_launch, WI-javoh; net_listen, WI-nosah): each time, the values the help lost are exactly the ones the axis gained since the epilog was last touched.\n\nWHY THIS IS THE ROOT CAUSE AND NOT THE SYMPTOM. An author adding a boundary value edits the registry, the catalogue rows and the consumers, and has no reason to suspect a prose block in cli.py restates the same closed set. A restated vocabulary drifts by default and is only corrected by someone reading both copies side by side; no test compares the epilog to the registry.\n\nTHE FIX. Build the epilog's list from `IO_BOUNDARY_TYPES` at parser-construction time, the way the validator's error message already does, so the closed set has one home. The prose around it stays hand-written; only the enumeration is derived.\n\nSCOPE NOTE, so this is not over-fixed: the claim to make true is \"the enumeration is derived\", not \"no help text may contain a list\". A worked example or an \"e.g.\" is fine and should stay.\n\nRELATED, CITE DO NOT FOLD: INV-rotup (META: CLI help text and README claims drift from current behavior) is the class item and is `satisfied`. Its enforcement -- test_cli_docs_prose_gate.py -- covers a removed-feature denylist, README invocation parsing, and a committed FLAG matrix. None of those looks at the VALUES a flag's argument may take, so this instance is outside the gate's scope rather than a gate that failed. Whether that item's status survives the new shape is the owner's call, not folded here.\nRevised 2026-09-30 from the premise check (see discussion)."  # 056f
- op: discuss  # 6f18
  at: '2026-10-02T00:35:26Z'  # 6f18
  by: agent  # 6f18
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 6f18
  clock: 5  # 6f18
  nonce: 6f18  # 6f18
  message: "DONE ON LOCAL BRANCH (concurrent run 09-30, not pushed): jgstern-agent/fix/jizil-derive-boundary-help, commit 46b6b8e492 \"fix(cli): derive verify-claims --help boundary list from the registry\".\n\nChange: cli.py gains _verify_claims_boundary_vocabulary_help(), which builds the help's boundary enumeration from io_boundary_types.all_io_boundary_names() (the definition of KNOWN_IO_BOUNDARIES, the set load_claims validates against) when the parser is constructed, sorted as the validator's error prints it. The hand-written list in the YAML comment column is gone; the help points to docs/concept-axes.md (generated from the registry) for meanings rather than restating them. No per-value glosses were written, so nothing in the help restates audit 0021's logging/ipc_send split.\n\nTests: test_cli_docs_prose_gate.py gains a 4th check (3 tests): rendered help list == all_io_boundary_names() == KNOWN_IO_BOUNDARIES with navigation_read named; a monkeypatched registry (navigation_read removed, a probe added) changes the rendered help; every listed value loads through load_claims and not_a_real_boundary raises ClaimsFileError.\n- red before fix: 3 failed, 28 passed (heading absent)\n- green: smart-test targeted, 260 files, 7594 passed / 0 failed / 3 skipped / 13 xfailed; changed source files at 100% coverage\n- mutation (old 20-name list restored under the new heading): 2 failed (navigation_read missing; probe missing), 29 passed; restored.\n\nLive repro on the branch: verify-claims --help lists 21 values incl. navigation_read (was 0 occurrences); the help list equals the validator's \"valid boundaries:\" list element for element (21/21, same order); boundary: navigation_read -> confirmed rc 0 on a one-file fixture with a call edge; boundary: not_a_real_boundary -> \"Error: unknown boundary ...\" rc 2.\n\nFamily sweep (cli.py only, io-boundary axis): cmd_io_boundaries docstring lists exactly the 17 counts_in_headline values (accurate); cmd_verify_claims docstring source list matches AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP keys; --io-boundary / --boundary help and the io-boundaries epilog give examples (\"e.g.\", parenthetical), not closed sets. Not checked: other axes' vocabularies in other help epilogs; README/docs (the spec says \"the io-boundaries vocabulary\" without enumerating)."  # 6f18
